# Commit Message Conventions

This governs every git commit message written in this repository, by a person or by an agent. Pull request titles are out of scope.

---

## Format

- The subject follows Conventional Commits: `type(scope): subject`, with a scope when one fits.
- Allowed types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `build`, `ci`, `chore`, `revert`.
- The whole subject line is between {{.Commit.Min}} and {{.Commit.Max}} characters, type and scope included.
- The subject is in the imperative mood, lowercase after the colon, with no trailing period.
- A body is optional. Separate it with a blank line and explain why, not what the diff shows.

## Atomic and cohesive

- One logical change per commit: a phase of work, not its smallest step.
- Stage by explicit path, never `git add -A` or `git add .`, and review the staged diff.
- Every commit leaves the repository working.
- Never commit secrets or real environment files.
- Keep refactors, renames and behavior changes in separate commits.

---

## Non-goals

- Does not dictate branch names or pull request titles.
